&1\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}$ has only one independent eigenvector. Is it diagonalizable?","options":["Yes, because it is triangular.","No, it does not have enough independent eigenvectors.","No, because no triangular matrix is diagonalizable.","Yes, because it has an eigenvalue."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f1d434f5bbf5e76aed6f2e12","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"la_batch18_diagonalizable_matrix_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $P^{-1}AP=\\operatorname{diag}(4,-1)$ for an invertible $P$, what can be said about $A$?","options":["$A$ is not square.","$A$ is the zero matrix.","$A$ is diagonalizable.","$A$ has no eigenvalues."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"54a573d84e2f0a8fd2f6d124","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"la_batch18_diagonalizable_matrix_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"A $2\\times2$ matrix has two independent eigenvectors. What follows?","options":["It must have determinant $0$.","It has no diagonal form.","It is diagonalizable.","It is defective."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0c11738ac236cfd63cf3979c","time_limit":20,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"la_batch18_diagonalizable_matrix_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the connection between diagonalizable matrices and eigenbases?","options":["A matrix is diagonalizable exactly when no eigenvectors exist.","A matrix is diagonalizable exactly when an eigenbasis exists.","Diagonalizability means the standard basis is always an eigenbasis.","An eigenbasis exists only for singular matrices."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1655484bafe867565b2bcfd4","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"la_batch18_diagonalizable_matrix_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A $4\\times4$ matrix has four distinct eigenvalues. How many independent eigenvectors are guaranteed?","options":["$1$","$4$","$2$","$3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"365a274a7d92576d101964fa","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"la_batch18_diagonalizable_matrix_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A $3\\times3$ matrix has only two independent eigenvectors in total. Can it be diagonalizable?","options":["No, because diagonalizable matrices have no eigenvectors.","Yes, if one eigenvalue is zero.","No, it needs three independent eigenvectors.","Yes, two are enough in dimension three."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"286847a93a02f6ac60c92532","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"la_batch18_diagonalizable_matrix_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does diagonalizability preserve about $A$ when passing to $D=P^{-1}AP$?","options":["The columns of $A$ become zero.","All entries of $A$ are preserved exactly.","Eigenvalues are preserved under similarity.","The rank must become $1$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d426852f92fac718972ca5e2","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"la_batch18_diagonalizable_matrix_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is diagonalizability useful for powers of a matrix?","options":["It changes eigenvalues into determinants.","It avoids multiplication only for $k=1$.","It makes every power equal to $I$.","If $A=PDP^{-1}$, then powers reduce to $A^k=PD^kP^{-1}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2396e30332d1e0b20fbd5106","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"la_batch18_diagonalizable_matrix_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"If $A=P\\operatorname{diag}(2,3)P^{-1}$, what is $A^4$?","options":["$P\\operatorname{diag}(2,3)P^{-1}$","$P\\operatorname{diag}(16,81)P^{-1}$","$P^4\\operatorname{diag}(2,3)P^{-4}$","$P\\operatorname{diag}(8,12)P^{-1}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9250560566a99122612bab9d","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"no_diagnostic_value"}]}}